The count map makes each update quick. Every time a number is seen, its count increases, and the current winner is checked immediately. The smaller-value rule handles ties without needing a second pass.

This assumes the input contains at least one value, which is the usual problem condition.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int mostFrequentElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_map<int, int> count;
        int answer = nums[0];
        int highest = 0;

        for (int number : nums) {
            count[number]++;

            if (count[number] > highest ||
                (count[number] == highest && number < answer)) {
                highest = count[number];
                answer = number;
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average.
Space complexity: `O(u)`, where `u` is the number of distinct values.

This keeps the logic very direct. For each different value, the code counts how many times it appears and compares that count with the current answer. A set prevents checking the same value repeatedly.

Dry run for `nums = [5, 1, 5, 1, 1]`: value `5` appears twice, while value `1` appears three times, so the result is `1`. An empty list returns `-1`.

```python
class Solution:
    def mostFrequentElement(self, nums):
        if not nums:
            return -1

        best_value = nums[0]
        best_count = 0
        checked = set()

        for value in nums:
            if value in checked:
                continue

            checked.add(value)
            count = 0
            for item in nums:
                if item == value:
                    count += 1

            if count > best_count or (count == best_count and value < best_value):
                best_count = count
                best_value = value

        return best_value
```

Time complexity: `O(n * u)`, where `u` is the number of different values. In the worst case this is `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(u)` for the set of checked values.

Sorting puts equal numbers next to each other. After that, one scan can count each group. The answer is updated when a group is larger than the previous best, or when it has the same size but a smaller value.

The input array is changed because it is sorted in place. If the array is empty, the code returns `-1`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int mostFrequentElement(int[] nums) {
        if (nums.length == 0) {
            return -1;
        }

        java.util.Arrays.sort(nums);

        int bestValue = nums[0];
        int bestCount = 0;
        int index = 0;

        while (index < nums.length) {
            int value = nums[index];
            int end = index;

            while (end < nums.length && nums[end] == value) {
                end++;
            }

            int count = end - index;
            if (count > bestCount) {
                bestCount = count;
                bestValue = value;
            }

            index = end;
        }

        return bestValue;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because of sorting.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space apart from the sorting implementation.

`Counter` is useful here because it builds all frequencies in one line. The second step chooses the value with the largest count. Using `(count, -value)` makes a smaller value win when counts are equal.

Small dry run: for `[7, 3, 7, 3, 3]`, the counts are `7: 2` and `3: 3`, so `3` is returned. If every number appears once, the smallest number is selected.

```python
from collections import Counter

class Solution:
    def mostFrequentElement(self, nums):
        if not nums:
            return -1

        counts = Counter(nums)
        return max(counts, key=lambda value: (counts[value], -value))
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average.
Space complexity: `O(u)` for the counter, where `u` is the number of distinct values.

A hash map keeps the count for each value. While counting, the code also remembers the best value seen so far. If two values have the same count, the smaller value is kept.

For example, with `nums = [4, 2, 4, 3, 2, 4]`, the counts become `4 -> 3`, `2 -> 2`, and `3 -> 1`, so the answer is `4`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int mostFrequentElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_map<int, int> frequency;
        int bestValue = nums[0];
        int bestCount = 0;

        for (int value : nums) {
            int currentCount = ++frequency[value];

            if (currentCount > bestCount ||
                (currentCount == bestCount && value < bestValue)) {
                bestCount = currentCount;
                bestValue = value;
            }
        }

        return bestValue;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the frequency map.

